WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.150
A spreadsheet formula can run, point to the wrong cells, and quietly return the wrong answer. FormulaBench is built for that risk.

2
00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:15.517
Qwen proposes the spreadsheet answer. FormulaBench controls what it sees, what it may change, and how correctness is judged.

3
00:00:15.517 --> 00:00:22.257
It begins with a plain-English request and the original workbook, plus the exact sheets and cells that need answers.

4
00:00:22.257 --> 00:00:29.676
FormulaBench reads formula text and cached values, then turns relevant sheets, ranges and nearby cells into a compact evidence pack.

5
00:00:29.676 --> 00:00:41.013
That evidence goes to Qwen three point eight, twenty-seven B, through Tinker: one logical sample, temperature zero, thinking disabled, and no model-level repair loop.

6
00:00:41.013 --> 00:00:48.223
Qwen interprets the request and proposes formulas or values for exact worksheet addresses. It does not freely operate Excel.

7
00:00:48.223 --> 00:00:59.299
Deterministic checks require complete coverage, permitted addresses and valid data types. A narrow static screen blocks explicit external references and defined executable functions.

8
00:00:59.299 --> 00:01:09.748
A valid response is written into a fresh workbook, then reopened to verify structure and assigned cells. A failure returns the original workbook unchanged, never partly edited.

9
00:01:09.748 --> 00:01:21.790
The production inference path never opens gold. After generation, LibreOffice recalculates the file, then a separate evaluator compares every target value with the golden answer.

10
00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:31.769
The frozen four-hundred-task result used untuned Qwen. It passed one hundred thirty-three tasks: thirty-three point two five per cent, with forty point five six per cent cell accuracy.

11
00:01:31.769 --> 00:01:43.080
Later, FormulaBench trained a rank-thirty-two LoRA on fifty-nine development examples for fifteen steps. It adds a compact correction, not a full retraining of twenty-seven billion parameters.

12
00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:55.488
On fifteen development-validation tasks, base Qwen passed seven and Qwen plus LoRA passed nine. That is real but limited: not a new four-hundred-task score, and not private-benchmark proof.
